--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Device-speed sensitivity probe — why "neutral" is not "safe"
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The identity above is a statement about THIS HOST, not about the product. Every
PAP-1659 branch is unreachable here only because host runtimes are 1-2 orders of
magnitude under the 45000ms budget. Device runtimes are not.

Established device gap (PAP-1682 / PAP-1677, Sentry, n=160 FP5 events): Hermes on
device runs this pipeline ~33-38x slower than V8 on this host. Host median 1197ms
x 33-38 = 39.5-45.5s. The device median photo therefore sits ON the 45s deadline,
not comfortably under it.

Scaling every row of pap1675_rows_9c4eacc.csv by a uniform factor f and asking
how many photos reach the 45000ms budget:

  f      photos tripping   of which currently   Reading A   Reading B   answer
         the deadline      correct  abstain  cw   (corr/N)   (corr/ans)   rate
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   1x        0/362 (0.0%)      -       -     -      58.0%      89.0%     65.2%
  10x        2/362 (0.6%)      2       0     0      57.5%      88.8%     64.6%
  15x       24/362 (6.6%)     17       6     1      53.3%      88.5%     60.2%
  20x       66/362(18.2%)     50      14     2      44.2%      87.0%     50.8%
  30x      117/362(32.3%)     81      30     6      35.6%      86.6%     41.2%
  33x      136/362(37.6%)     88      37    11      33.7%      89.1%     37.8%
  36x      170/362(47.0%)     99      55    16      30.7%      91.7%     33.4%
  38x      186/362(51.4%)    103      65    18      29.6%      93.0%     31.8%

  Inside the measured 33-38x device band the gate would convert 88-103 photos
  that are CORRECT today into abstains, and 11-18 confidently-wrong ones into
  abstains. Net: Reading A 58.0% -> 29.6-33.7% (-24 to -28pp), answer rate
  65.2% -> 31.8-37.8%, while Reading B moves +0.1 to +4.0pp because the gate
  strips correct and wrong answers at roughly similar rates and the surviving
  fast photos skew easy.

  Per bucket at the band edges, photos tripping / of which correct today:
              33x                38x
  Small  9-15T   67/136, 55 corr    75/136, 56 corr
  Mid    16-20T   9/ 33,  8 corr    12/ 33,  9 corr
  Large  21-28T  32/113, 15 corr    52/113, 22 corr
  XL     29-60T  28/ 80, 10 corr    47/ 80, 16 corr
  Small gears are hit hardest in absolute correct-answers-lost — they are both
  the largest bucket and the most accurate one today.

  BOUNDS AND CAVEATS — this is a bounding estimate, not a measurement:
   - It over-counts. `runtime` here is whole-analyze wall time; the PAP-1689
     ruling scopes the budget to stageMs.detect+methods, and the deadline is
     anchored AFTER decode+preprocess (PAP-1683 fix B). Decode+preprocess is
     inside the scaled number but outside the budget clock.
   - It assumes a uniform scale factor. The Hermes/V8 gap is not uniform across
     stages; typed-array inner loops degrade worse than allocation-light code.
   - It under-counts in one direction: some deadline branches abstain mid-count
     rather than at entry, so a photo can trip a guard and still be slower than
     modeled here.
   - It says nothing about b137 field behaviour at the previous 5000ms budget,
     where the gate reportedly abstained on 100% of real FP5 photos. That
     observation and this table are consistent: 5000ms is ~4x under the device
     median, 45000ms is ~1x it.

  What this licenses: the corpus CANNOT be used to size a wall-clock budget, in
  either direction. It could not detect a 5000ms budget that abstains on
  everything on device, and it cannot certify 45000ms as safe. The only
  instrument that can is device telemetry — the `budgetExhausted` flag in the
  b137+ Sentry debug_report events. That is a separate, cheap measurement and it
  is filed rather than done here.

